  - On starting the first game the program must print a greeting 'Welcome to Prime' (or 'Welcome to
  [game name]' for new games) and indicate which player is to move.
  - list-games must list currently running games (not ended) in ascending creation order as lines
  '[game name]: [turns]'.
  - On victory the program must print '[player name] wins!' and output the winning path.
  - The second command-line name selects the AI: if it matches a computer-player name that AI plays;
   otherwise the second player is human.
  - A successful place command must not print a confirmation; instead it should print '[player
  name]'s turn' for the next player, unless there is no next turn.
  - A successful swap command must print '[player name] swaps'.
  - After handling an input the program must wait for further inputs (i.e., remain interactive).
  - Prime must be started from the command line with three or four arguments: board size, player1
  name (Red), player2 name (Blue), and optional auto-print.
  - Prime must terminate the program when the user inputs the quit command.
  - The initial game started by the program must be named 'Prime'.
  - The program must not use System.exit() or other excluded functions to implement quitting.
  - After printing an error message the program must continue and wait for the next input.
  - new-game must start a new game analogous to a normal start and print a greeting that includes
  the game's name.
  - Game names must be non-empty, contain no whitespace characters, and must be unique.
  - If the first command-line name matches a computer player's name the game must not start and the
  program exits with an error.
  - The optional fourth startup parameter may only be the literal auto-print; if absent the board is
   not auto-printed before each move.
  - The program should be designed so that adding new computer players is easy for future
  developers.
  - The history command outputs entries in the format '[player name]: [x coordinate] [y coordinate];
   without a parameter turns defaults to 1; turns must be a positive integer.
  - In printed board lines each hexagon is separated from the next by a single space.
  - Player names must be non-empty and able to be passed via the command line; otherwise there are
  no restrictions.
  - Player names passed on the command line must be unique; if they are not unique the program exits
   with an error message.
  - Interactive user interface outputs must match exactly the specified text including case, spaces,
