● Injected adaptive memory context into system prompt

● <adaptive_rules>
  - `switch-game` must print `Switched to [game name]` on success and may switch to finished games
  to output their state.
  - If the second player name equals a computer-player name, that AI is selected; otherwise the
  second player is human.
  - `new-game` must start a new game analogous to a normal start and print a greeting that contains
  the new game's name.
  - A successful `swap` command must output `[player name] swaps`.
  - Game names must be non-empty, contain no whitespace characters, and must be unique (no
  duplicates).
  - The `swap` command does not count as a move and must not increase the `list-games` turns
  counter.
  - The program should be designed to make it easy for future developers to add new computer players
   (AI extensibility).
  - `list-games` must list currently running games (not ended) sorted ascending by creation time in
  the schema `[game name]: [turns]`.
  - `history` must use the format `[player name]: [x coordinate] [y coordinate]`; the optional
  `[turns]` parameter defaults to 1 and must be a positive integer.
  - Player names may be any non-empty string that can be passed via the command line.
  - The fourth command-line parameter is optional and the only valid value is `auto-print`; if
  omitted the board is not auto-printed before each move.
  - Assignment submission may only use java.util.Scanner and elements from the listed java.lang,
  java.io, java.util, java.util.regex, java.util.stream, java.util.function, java.time, and
  java.time.format packages.
  - Assignment submission must be individual work (Einzelarbeit).
  - Assignment submission must choose appropriate visibilities for classes, methods, and attributes.
  - Assignment submission must compile without errors.
  - Assignment submission must follow all whitespace, variable, method, and package naming rules.
  - Assignment submission must follow the Javadoc documentation rules required by the assignment.
  - Assignment submission must not call System.exit(), Runtime.exit() or similar.
  - Assignment submission must not use the default package.
  - Assignment submission must obey a maximum source code line width of 120 characters.
  - Assignment submission must pass the mandatory tests before being considered successful.
  - Assignment submission must satisfy all other Checkstyle rules enforced by the submission system.
  - Assignment submission must upload Java source (*.java) files for task A in the corresponding
  directory and folder structure.
  - Assignment submission must use Java SE 17 exclusively.
  - Assignment submission will be awarded zero points if any of the listed mandatory rules are
  violated.
  - The `quit` command must work in every situation because many tests use it to terminate the
  program.
  - Choose meaningful names for all identifiers in the submission.
  - Comments must be written consistently either in English or in German.
  - Padding: the n-th printed line must have n-1 leading spaces (first line zero, second one, etc.).
  - Some Checkstyle rules are specially marked as mandatory and will cause a submission to receive
  zero points if violated.
  - The Javadoc @author tag must contain only the student's u-K␀rzel.
  - Program source code must be written in English.
  - The `help` command must list commands alphabetically, each line formatted `* [command name]:
  [command description]`.
  - Error messages may be written in English and should be meaningful to explain why input was
  rejected.
  - Error messages must not contain special characters such as newlines or German umlauts.
  - Every error message must start with the literal prefix "Error:".
  - After processing an input the program must wait for further inputs (interactive loop).
  - Board size must be a positive odd integer greater than 4 and must not exceed 12345.
  - If the first player name equals the name of an AI, the game may not be started and must exit
  with an error message.
  - Player names must be unique; if they are not the program terminates with an error message.
  - Programs must never reassign the standard input or output streams.
  - Programs must use System.in for input unless the task explicitly specifies otherwise.
  - Programs must use System.out for standard output unless the task explicitly specifies otherwise.
  - The game must be started from the command line with three or four arguments: side length,
  player1 name (Red), player2 name (Blue), optional `auto-print`.
  - The initial (first) game must be named `Prime`.
  - The program must exit at any time when the user inputs the command `quit`.
  - When user input does not match the required format the program must output an error message
  explaining why the input was rejected and then continue waiting for the next input.
  - Interactive user interface outputs must exactly match the specification including case, spaces,
  and line breaks.
  - Interactive user interfaces must output only the information specified in the assignment and no
  additional information.
  </adaptive_rules>

